2705078125, -0.005908966064453125, -0.0244140625, 0.01248931884765625, -0.028411865234375, -0.0110626220703125, 0.0245513916015625, 0.00504302978515625, -0.01105499267578125, 0.00490570068359375, 0.018524169921875, 0.0167999267578125, 0.0018320083618164062, -0.041961669921875, -0.0191192626953125, -0.03643798828125, 0.00382232666015625, 0.0023746490478515625, 0.0165557861328125, 0.002185821533203125, -0.0161285400390625, 0.0111083984375, -0.00592041015625, -0.017303466796875, 0.001277923583984375, -0.0179290771484375, -0.0259857177734375, 0.01641845703125, 0.00351715087890625, -0.01202392578125, 0.0012273788452148438, 0.01617431640625, -0.015289306640625, 0.0031070709228515625, -0.01256561279296875, -0.00994873046875, -0.02899169921875, 0.020751953125, 0.01348114013671875, -0.02423095703125, -0.003269195556640625, 0.0223846435546875, -0.0198516845703125, 0.0283355712890625, 0.001735687255859375, -0.00394439697265625, 0.01690673828125, -0.00815582275390625, 0.00860595703125, -0.0074920654296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns\u2014recurring arrangements of phase relations or synchrony patterns distributed across space and time\u2014is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. Traditional approaches, such as standard phase coherence and permutation entropy, tend to summarize global synchrony or temporal complexity, but cannot distinguish between different spatial arrangements with similar global metrics. In contrast, our framework offers finer granularity by directly encoding local spatial relations among oscillator phases using extended spatial ordinal patterns, thereby capturing phase gradients, wave fronts, and local clusters within a unified symbolic description.\n\nOur approach operates as follows: for each oscillator, we examine its phase relative to immediate spatial neighbors, constructing symbolic patterns according to their relative ordering. A crucial innovation is the explicit handling of scenarios where neighboring phases are nearly equal. We operationalize \u201cnearly equal\u201d using a fixed phase difference threshold \u03b4\u03b8 (e.g., \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians in our experiments): if |\u03b8i \u2212 \u03b8j| < \u03b4\u03b8 for neighboring oscillators i and j, we assign a dedicated symbol that encodes this tie. This threshold can be tuned based on data granularity or noise levels and affords reproducibility across settings.\n\nThe method\u2019s flexibility in spatial scale is achieved by adjusting the neighborhood size: one may analyze immediate neighbors (nearest-neighbor coupling) or larger neighborhoods, depending on the underlying network topology (e.g., rings, 2D grids, or arbitrary graphs). For each oscillator and its chosen neighborhood, we derive a local symbolic pattern at each time point; the collection of these across the network defines the instantaneous spatial ordinal pattern distribution. From this, we compute spatial permutation entropy at the whole-network or local scales, quantifying the diversity of spatial patterns with parameters explicitly specified (e.g., neighborhood radius k, network adjacency).\n\nWe validate our framework using synthetic networks of coupled oscillators with precisely controlled parameters: each network consists of either N = 100 phase oscillators arranged in a ring or 2D grid, governed by the Kuramoto model with adjustable coupling strength, noise amplitude, and intrinsic frequency dispersion. Transitions between synchrony, chimera, and traveling wave regimes are induced by systematically varying coupling and adding phase perturbations. We detect these regime transitions by tracking spatial permutation entropy, observing abrupt entropy shifts at known transition points. Statistical significance of performance is assessed by comparing the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC AUC) for our measure (e.g., AUC = 0.93 for chimera detection) versus conventional phase coherence (AUC = 0.67), demonstrating superior sensitivity and specificity.\n\nTo classify dynamical regimes, we apply data-driven entropy thresholds set via maximizing classification accuracy on validation sets. This yields quantitative metrics: for example, regime classification using our entropy achieved >90% accuracy across simulated trials, with thresholds selected to optimize cross-validated performance.\n\nThe method is further demonstrated on resting-state EEG recordings from 15 human volunteers (10 eyes-open, 10 eyes-closed sessions per subject). EEG data were preprocessed by standard bandpass filtering and artifact rejection. Spatial permutation entropy was computed for each time window, using physical adjacency between electrodes to define neighborhoods, and \u03b4\u03b8 set as in simulations. For each subject, condition-specific entropy distributions were compared using non-parametric tests, and a logistic regression classifier was trained to discriminate conditions (mean cross-validated classification accuracy = 0.81, balanced accuracy reported alongside sensitivity and specificity). This shows robust detection of subtle, condition-dependent modulations of spatial phase organization, not visible in global EEG synchrony metrics.\n\nFinally, while our method is sensitive to a rich variety of spatiotemporal organization across different systems, it has limitations: entropy values can be affected by noise, and the choice of threshold \u03b4\u03b8 and neighborhood size can influence sensitivity and specificity; these parameters should be selected based on the context and validated via sensitivity analyses. Generalization to networks with highly irregular topology or strong measurement noise may require adaptation of symbol assignment rules or noise-robust statistical estimation.\n\nIn summary, spatial permutation entropy computed from extended spatial ordinal patterns provides a reproducible, fine-grained, and sensitive measure for tracking spatiotemporal organization and detecting regime transitions in both model and empirical oscillatory systems, with explicit methodological parameters and transparent limitations."}}